    "sample_id": "test:3409",
    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we first need to identify the units digits of the individual powers of 3. Let's observe the pattern in the units digits of the first few powers of 3:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 & = 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^2 & = 9 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^3 & = 27 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^4 & = 81 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n3^5 & = 243 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^6 & = 729 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^7 & = 2187 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^8 & = 6561 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, the units digits of the powers of 3 cycle as follows: \\(3, 9, 7, 1\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine how many terms in the sequence \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\) are there. This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the largest \\(n\\) such that \\(2n - 1 \\leq 2009\\):\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 2009 \\implies 2n = 2010 \\implies n = 1005\n\\]\n\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the sequence. To find the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we need to consider the units digits of these terms. The units digits repeat every 4 terms, so we can group the terms into sets of 4:\n\n\\[\n(3, 9, 7, 1), (3, 9, 7, 1), \\ldots, (3, 9, 7, 1)\n\\]\n\nEach group of 4 terms has a sum of units digits:\n\n\\[\n3 + 9 + 7 + 1 = 20 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 0)}\n\\]\n\nSince there are 1005 terms, we have \\(1005 / 4 = 251\\) full groups and 1 additional term. The sum of the units digits of the 251 full groups is:\n\n\\[\n251 \\times 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nThe units digit of the additional term \\(3^{2009}\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(3^1\\), which is 3. Therefore, the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n0 + 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the units digit of the sum is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
